      "source_text": "A complete three-point example. Let $D = \\{0, 1, 2\\} \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ with $d(x,x') = |x - x'|$ and the kernel given by its Gram matrix $K = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0\\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{pmatrix}$ (positive definite: leading minors $1, 1, 1$ (see Primer A)). Take $f = -2\\,k(\\cdot,1)$, i.e. $\\big(f(0), f(1), f(2)\\big) = (0, -2, -4)$ with $\\|f\\|_k = 2 = B_{\\mathrm{true}}$; this is the extremal function of (a) for the data point $0$ and $x = 1$. Let $h = -3$, so $0$ and $1$ are safe and $2$ is not; $L = 2$ is the exact Lipschitz constant; seed $S_0 = \\{0, 1\\}$ (two safe points, as in the caption of Table 1); run Real-$\\beta$-SafeOpt noise-free with $B_{\\mathrm{est}} = 0.5$.",
